  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "DMA outstanding capacity, not DRAM, limits streaming kernels in the RTL reference.",
      "detail": "With 16 outstanding 64-bit beats and ~90 ns DRAM latency the DMA path sustains 1.23 GB/s against 8.96 GB/s of DDR4-3200 x32. Covering the bandwidth-delay product of the recommended memory needs >= 51 128-bit beats in flight; the recommendation uses 64."
    },
    {
      "title": "A single shared DMA engine deadlocks against its own accelerator.",
      "detail": "tb_system hung when an accelerator->SRAM (S2MM) channel occupied the only read engine while the operands it was waiting for were queued behind it. sf_dma now has independent MM2S/MEM2MEM and S2MM engines sharing one channel table; tb_dma, tb_system and tb_deadlock pass on the fixed RTL."
    },
    {
      "title": "Joined operand streams need FIFO depth >= DMA burst length (or sequenced loads).",
      "detail": "tb_deadlock: with a 4-entry vector-unit FIFO and 16-beat bursts the DMA stalls after 4 beats and the watchdog fires; with 16 entries all 256 result beats complete. Predicate joined_stream_deadlock(4,16)=True."
    },
    {
      "title": "Bank-aligned SRAM-to-SRAM copies resonate into read/write bank conflicts.",
      "detail": "RTL: a 4 KiB SPM->SPM copy with 16-beat bursts takes 652 cycles, but 556 cycles when the destination is skewed by one beat. XOR interleave or buffer skew removes it; the Python cycle model matches the skewed case but not the aligned resonance (listed as a model limitation)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Power-of-two strides: XOR interleave fixes strides up to the fold width only.",
      "detail": "RTL tb_spm (4 banks, 3 ports): stride 4 throughput 1.50 (modulo) -> 2.39 (XOR); stride 16 gives 1.00 with the 2-bit XOR fold. Block interleave is worst for streaming (1.23 at stride 1, 8 banks) and best for random traffic (2.63 requests/cycle)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Accumulator double-buffering is worth ~1.8x on the tensor array when K equals the drain length.",
      "detail": "RTL tb_accel, K=32: 88.6 % vs 49.7 % MAC utilisation. The analytical tile model (per tile max(K, drain) with double-buffering, K + drain without) reproduces RTL to 0.0 % after fitting its 1-3 cycle pipeline fill to these runs; the per-tile slope is not fitted."
    },
    {
      "title": "Response-credit depth must cover the 4-cycle SPM round trip.",
      "detail": "RTL: RSP_DEPTH=2 gives 0.50 vs 1.00 requests/cycle single-port throughput with RSP_DEPTH=4, exactly as Little's law predicts (credits / round trip)."
    }
  ],
